findings.claim:
Orientation and scope triage are pure VLM calls today: four rotated 1024 px JPEGs, plus one short triage call in phase 1. The repo already has an OCR-based orientation scorer and a regex for Russian document titles. With a fast line OCR (PP-OCRv5 eslav, or PaddleOCR-VL Spotting), the pipeline can:
- choose the rotation with the most horizontal text and the header above the footer;
- read the heading literally with a box;
- record out-of-scope forms (ТН-2, CMR, счёт-фактура, декларация) as unknown with that heading when two frames give the same heading.
It falls back to the VLM whenever the OCR result is ambiguous. It must never hard-gate a target form to unknown.

The production stack is llama.cpp CUDA with 26 of 32 GB in use. llama.cpp mtmd now supports PaddleOCR-VL (merged Feb 2026), dots.ocr (merged Apr 9, 2026), GLM-OCR, DeepSeek-OCR and HunyuanOCR, with ggml-org GGUFs. Running one of them on port 8091 needs no vLLM on sm_120. Choice by memory:
- PaddleOCR-VL-1.5, HunyuanOCR-1.5 and MonkeyOCRv2 fit in the remaining ~6 GB.
- dots.ocr fits only at Q8 and tightly.
- Chandra-2, Qianfan-OCR and olmOCR-2 would need the main model's 65536 context reduced, or a model swap.
Known llama.cpp caveats for PaddleOCR-VL:
- no layout stage, so PP-DocLayoutV3 must run separately;
- the prompt needs a `<__media__>OCR:` marker;
- one image per request;
- the ggml blog flags possibly degraded quality.
If vLLM is needed, PaddleOCR publishes a `latest-nvidia-gpu-sm120` vLLM image (CUDA 12.9+), verified only on RTX 5070. SGLang and FastDeploy are documented for compute capability below 12.0 only.

Qwen3.x uses 16 px patches with 2x2 merge, so one visual token is 32x32 px. With `--image-min-tokens 1024`, llama.cpp enlarges any image under 1,048,576 px. Today the 576x1024 context (589,824 px) is shrunk with Lanczos on the client, then enlarged by the server to about 768x1376 (1032 tokens). That costs the same tokens as sending 768x1376 from native pixels but carries about 1.78x less real detail. Small native crops are also enlarged: a 1080x230 caption-and-value crop goes from about 238 tokens to about 1050. The model therefore sees magnified crops, contrary to ZOOM_INSTRUCTIONS and README.md:213. Settings to try:
- context 768x1376 (1032 tokens) built from the native frame;
- `--image-min-tokens 256`, so crops keep their own size and upload_pixel_size matches what the model sees;
- `--image-max-tokens 4096`, under which a full 2160x3840 frame becomes 1536x2720 = 4080 tokens.

Each orientation call sends four rotated 1024-px contexts, each enlarged by the server to about 1032 tokens, so at least 4.1k image tokens per call. Orientation can be decided at 224x224: PaddleOCR's 4-class document orientation model reports 99.06% top-1. Options, from most to least compatible with the current design:
- send the 4 rotations at 288x512 (144 tokens each) once min-tokens is lowered;
- fold rotation into the existing one-call triage;
- use PP-LCNet_x1_0_doc_ori, a separate classifier, which needs customer approval.
